 Alert Rules in Prometheus

🔹 What are Alert Rules? 🚨
 Alert rules define when Prometheus should trigger an alert based on metric conditions.
They help systems automatically detect problems instead of waiting for someone to notice them manually.
👉 “If this condition happens, send an alert.”

🔹 Why are Alert Rules Important? 🏗️
In production, engineers cannot watch dashboards 24/7 👀
Alert rules help by:
✔ Detecting issues automatically
 ✔ Sending notifications quickly 📩
 ✔ Reducing downtime ⏱️
 ✔ Improving system reliability 🚀

🔹 How Alerts are Defined: ⚡
An alert rule usually contains:
✔ Alert Name → Identifier
 ✔ Expression (expr) → Condition logic
 ✔ Duration (for) → How long condition must remain true

🔹 Basic Alert Rule Example: 📊
alert: HighCPUUsage
expr: cpu_usage > 90
for: 5m

What this means:
👉 If CPU usage stays above 90% for 5 minutes
 → Trigger alert 🚨

🔹 Common Alert Conditions: 🔍
✔ High CPU usage
 ✔ Low memory available
 ✔ High API latency
 ✔ Increased error rate
 ✔ Server downtime

🔹 Real-world Examples: 💡
👉 CPU Alert
cpu_usage > 85
Trigger if server CPU becomes too high.

👉 Error Rate Alert
rate(http_errors_total[5m]) > 10
Trigger when application errors suddenly increase.

👉 Instance Down Alert
up == 0
Trigger when service becomes unavailable.

🔹 Real-world Scenario: 🌍
Your application receives heavy traffic 📈
CPU rises to 95% for several minutes.
👉 Alert rule detects threshold breach
 👉 Prometheus triggers alert
 👉 Notification sent to engineers 🚨
Result → Faster response and reduced outage.

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Alert rules = Automated monitoring 🚨
 ✔ Based on conditions & thresholds 📊
 ✔ Use expressions (expr) 🔍
 ✔ Help detect failures quickly ⚡
